    {
        "id": "8daaaca8f92fe129",
        "type": "comment",
        "z": "deca1f169edbc456",
        "name": "Example 03: JSON, keys and headers - select me and open the Info sidebar",
        "info": "## JSON, keys and headers\n\nA Kafka message has a **value** (`msg.payload`), an optional **key** (`msg.key`) and optional **headers** (`msg.headers`), small name/value pairs for metadata.\n\n**Before you deploy:** the example connects to a Kafka broker on `localhost:9092`. If yours runs elsewhere, double-click any Kafka node, click the pencil next to **Broker** (the configuration node *Local Kafka (example 03)*) and change **Brokers**. Then click **Deploy**.\n\n**Topics:** the producer creates `example-orders` with its first message if the broker allows automatic topic creation (Apache Kafka does by default, many managed services do not). Until a topic exists, the consumer shows `unknown topic/partition · retry …` (yellow) and keeps retrying, waiting a little longer each time; it starts at the first retry after the topic was created. To create topics yourself, use the admin node (example 06) or your Kafka tools.\n\n### Producer side\n\n- Objects and arrays in `msg.payload` are sent as JSON text.\n- The *change* node sets `msg.key` to the customer. Kafka sends messages with the same key to the same partition, so all orders of one customer stay in order.\n- It also sets `msg.headers`. The producer adds its own *static header* `source: node-red` (Message tab); a static header wins over a `msg.headers` entry with the same name. *Also send msg.headers* must be ticked for `msg.headers` to be used. Careful: *http in* and *http request* nodes put HTTP headers in `msg.headers`.\n\n### Consumer side\n\nThe consumer's *Payload* format is *Parsed JSON*, so `msg.payload` is an object again. The key and header values arrive as strings (choose *Buffer* on the Output tab for binary data).\n\nClick **send text that is not JSON**: the consumer cannot parse it, so it does not send it to its output. Instead a *catch* node receives the message, with the raw text in `msg.payload` and the reason in `msg.error`, and the consumer carries on with the next message.",
        "x": 340,
        "y": 40,
        "wires": []
    },
